{
  "id": "COMPOUND:32178 - PROTEIN:6218",
  "verbalization": "Cryptobeilic Acid B appears to interact with multiple targets that ultimately lead to the induction of the Induced myeloid leukemia cell differentiation protein Mcl-1. The evidence suggests that Cryptobeilic Acid B acts as a modulator of a signaling pathway involving taste receptor type 2 member 31, which is targeted by (-)-Rosmarinic Acid and (1R,4R,6S,8aR)-4,8a-dimethyl-6-propan-2-yl-1,2,3,6,7,8-hexahydronaphthalene-1,4-diol. These compounds, in turn, target Mcl-1, a key regulator of cell survival and differentiation in myeloid leukemia. The presence of multiple indirect pathways linking Cryptobeilic Acid B to Mcl-1 indicates a potential role in modulating cellular processes that contribute to leukemia progression. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, but further validation through direct interaction studies or in vivo experiments would be necessary to confirm the mechanistic link.",
